package builtin.decorators.Decorators


// ── Inheritance-based decorator foundation ──────────────────────────────────
//
// `Decorator` is a real, importable interface — not just a naming
// convention like the older `@Decorator`/`@Decorator` marking
// (`crates/snovalang/src/native/decorators.rs::is_decorator_defined`). It
// lives in `builtin/` (like `builtin.errors.Errors`/`builtin.metadata.
// Documented`) precisely so it ships with the language itself: any library
// — `stdlib.sonar.*`, a project's own package, a third-party one — can
// `import builtin.decorators.Decorators` and build its own decorator kit on
// top of it, without depending on any specific stdlib package.
//
// Same idiom already proven in `builtin.errors.Errors`
// (`interface Err { method message(): string ... }`, `class DomainError :
// Error { override method message(): string { ... } }`): an `interface`
// declares signatures only; a `struct`/`class` implements it by providing
// bodies, and `override` marks a body that satisfies a supertype's method.
//
//   interface Decorator {
//       method runBeforeExecution(): unit
//       method runAfterExecution(): unit
//   }
//
// Four kinds implement `Decorator`, one per thing a decorator can target.
// Each provides no-op bodies for the two shared hooks (so an author who only
// cares about one half of the pair doesn't have to override the other) and
// adds the 1-2 hooks specific to its own target:
//
//   FieldDecorator      — fields:      runOnGet / runOnSet
//   MethodDecorator     — methods:     runOnBind / runOnError
//   FunctionalDecorator — functions:   runInParallel
//   StructureDecorator  — classes/structs/interfaces: runOnInstanceCreated
//
// A concrete, author-written decorator extends one of the four kinds and
// carries its own state + a constructor that requires its own arguments —
// the same worked pattern already documented for `@Cache(ttl: 60)` in
// `stdlib.sonar.Decorators` (`decoratorArg`/`hasDecorator`):
//
//   @FunctionalDecorator
//   public struct Retry : FunctionalDecorator {
//       public let times: int
//       public let backoffMs: int
//
//       public method new(times: int, backoffMs: int) {
//           this.times = times
//           this.backoffMs = backoffMs
//       }
//
//       public override method runBeforeExecution(): unit {
//           Console.printline("Retry: up to " + times.toString() + " attempts")
//       }
//       public override method runAfterExecution(): unit {
//           Console.printline("Retry: attempt finished")
//       }
//       public override method runInParallel(): unit {
//           Console.printline("Retry: backing off " + backoffMs.toString() + "ms")
//       }
//   }
//
// This module only defines the shape. Actually *running* the hooks around a
// decorated target — and doing so "using events," publishing each
// transition on an event bus — is a library concern built on top of this
// foundation, not something this file does itself. `stdlib.sonar.
// DecoratorRuntime` (`libs/stdlib.sonar.async`) is one such library: it
// wires `Decorator` instances into `stdlib.sonar.Events`' `EventBus` and
// `stdlib.sonar.Pulsars`. It is an example, not the only way — any user
// library can import this file directly and wire its own runtime instead.
//
// As with `is_decorator_defined`, nothing here is enforced by the compiler:
// `extends`/`override`/`interface` parse but are not semantically checked
// today, so this stays a real, usable shape rather than a validated one.

public interface Decorator {
    method runBeforeExecution(): unit
    method runAfterExecution(): unit
}

// Base for decorators attached to a field. "Before/after" means before/after
// a read-or-write access; `runOnGet`/`runOnSet` distinguish which happened.
public struct FieldDecorator : Decorator {
    public override method runBeforeExecution(): unit {}
    public override method runAfterExecution(): unit {}

    // Fires when the decorated field is read.
    public method runOnGet(): unit {}

    // Fires when the decorated field is written.
    public method runOnSet(): unit {}
}

// Base for decorators attached to a method on a class/struct instance.
public struct MethodDecorator : Decorator {
    public override method runBeforeExecution(): unit {}
    public override method runAfterExecution(): unit {}

    // Fires once when this decorator instance is mounted onto a concrete
    // instance (the method-decorator equivalent of EventBus.mountDecorated).
    public method runOnBind(): unit {}

    // Fires when the decorated method's execution raises/produces an error.
    public method runOnError(): unit {}
}

// Base for decorators attached to a free function. Supersedes the earlier
// `@Interceptor` struct (beforeRun/runWhen/afterRun function-valued fields,
// previously in `stdlib.sonar.Events`) — same idea, expressed through
// inheritance and override instead of passing callback values.
public struct FunctionalDecorator : Decorator {
    public override method runBeforeExecution(): unit {}
    public override method runAfterExecution(): unit {}

    // Fires as a fire-and-forget side path, meant to be run on its own
    // pulsar by whichever runtime library invokes this decorator.
    public method runInParallel(): unit {}
}

// Base for decorators attached to a class, struct, or interface declaration
// itself (as opposed to one of its members).
public struct StructureDecorator : Decorator {
    public override method runBeforeExecution(): unit {}
    public override method runAfterExecution(): unit {}

    // Fires once per new instance of the decorated structure.
    public method runOnInstanceCreated(): unit {}
}
